///
/// 1. A second `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` subprocess call, comparing its (canonicalised) answer against
///    `root`.
/// 2. A pure filesystem check, `root.join(".git").exists()`, with no subprocess at all.
///
/// Both close every `ar86_floor` reproduction of C2 on their own terms. **Both were rejected**, not on grounds of
/// what they check, but of where: the full suite run found EITHER one deterministically broke
/// `brownfield::brownfield_adoption_end_to_end` (a held-out memory-retrieval regression the test's governance-suite
/// currency gate depends on started failing) -- reproduced eight times across both implementations and their
/// absence (3/3 pass with no early-return check ahead of `rev-list`, 5/5 fail with either one present, isolated
/// from every other change in this round by direct file-level bisection). The interaction was not fully isolated
/// within this round's budget: neither implementation ever actually disagreed with `root` in that test (a parallel
/// diagnostic confirmed zero mismatches), so the failure is not this check reaching a wrong answer -- it is some
/// other effect of an early-return existing here at all, in a part of the system (currency/continuity tracking)
/// this repair has no business touching. Rather than ship a fix whose blast radius is not understood, C2 is closed
/// a DIFFERENT way: [`FloorIdentity::project_identity`] is never derived from git at all, so a subdirectory's own
/// onboarding mints its OWN, different identity regardless of what lineage it reports -- independently verified
/// (`ar86_c2`) to still close the reproduction with NEITHER of the two lineage-side checks in place.
///
/// **AR86-C8.** Built with [`crate::util::constructed_env_command`], not a raw `std::process::Command::new("git")`:
/// this repository's identity must not be decided by a program resolved through the caller's ambient `PATH`, nor by
/// `GIT_DIR`/`GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`/`GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY` the caller's environment might carry — the same
/// reasoning every other subprocess on the execution path already follows. Measured to NOT be the cause of the
/// interaction above (the full suite passes with this alone, no toplevel check, present).
fn repository_lineage_id(root: &Path) -> String {
    let out = crate::util::constructed_env_command("git")
        .args(["rev-list", "--max-parents=0", "HEAD"])
        .current_dir(root)
        .output();
    let Ok(out) = out else {
        return "no-git-history".to_string();
    };
    if !out.status.success() {
        return "no-git-history".to_string();
    }
    let mut roots: Vec<String> = String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stdout)
        .lines()
        .map(|l| l.trim().to_string())
        .filter(|l| !l.is_empty())
        .collect();
    if roots.is_empty() {
        return "no-git-history".to_string();
    }
    roots.sort();
    roots.join(",")
}

/// **AR86-C1/C2/C3 (OD-P2-08 §5: "authenticate the authoritative transition").** The lineage above answers "is
/// this the SAME repository history?" — AR86-C1/C2 proved that is the wrong question: a `git clone` shares its
/// origin's lineage BY CONSTRUCTION, and any directory under a repository reports the SAME lineage (`git` walks
/// up), so lineage alone cannot tell "this project, as onboarded" apart from "a repository sharing its history,
/// laid out however the adversary likes". What actually distinguishes them is not a property of repository
/// CONTENT at all (content is exactly what a clone or a subdirectory legitimately reproduces) — it is which
/// checkout this repository's own onboarding transition actually happened in, and in what order this checkout's
/// own floor last advanced.
///
/// `FloorIdentity` is that anchor, kept OUTSIDE the sealed document so an ordinary copy of the floor FILE alone —
/// every AR86-C1/C2/C3/C4 reproduction — cannot carry it.
///
/// **P2-AR-0089 (AR88-C1/C1B/C2/C3/C3B/C9/C6): moved from [`STATE_DIR`] to the machine's own protected state.**
/// P2-AR-0088 proved, by execution, that "outside the sealed document" was not the same claim as "outside the
/// attacker's reach": `STATE_DIR` is *inside the project root*, declared exactly the same `class: operational,
/// mutation: os-only` as `governance/registry/**` itself (AR88-C9 — a classification, checked at task-close scope,
/// that an ordinary write simply never passes through), and writable by the SAME lower-trust party (an agent
/// operating in the repository) the floor document itself is. Copying both files (AR88-C1), or the cheaper move-
/// aside-and-let-it-bootstrap (AR88-C1B), or carrying the anchor out of a subdirectory (AR88-C2), or restoring an
/// earlier anchor alongside an earlier document (AR88-C3/C3B) all worked, silently, because the anchor was a
/// SECOND file in the SAME trust domain as the first — "raised attacker effort from one `cp` to two," never an
/// authentication boundary.
///
/// The anchor now lives under [`machine_floor_root`] — a new subdirectory of the SAME protected root
/// [`crate::srr::state::resolve_state_root`] already resolves for [`crate::t2`]'s own machine binding key
/// (`<state_root>/t2-binding/key.json`): outside every project, in the machine/administrator domain ARCH-0003 §7
/// already establishes for exactly this reason ("Clients persist the highest valid floors they have observed...
/// a property of the machine, not of one operation"). No ordinary project-scoped write reaches it — reused, not
/// reinvented; no new trusted authority, key, role or signer (OD-P2-08 §5, §8). Keyed by [`floor_anchor_key`]
/// (this checkout's own canonical path, never a value the document carries), so:
///
/// * a clone at a different path (same or a different machine) has no entry there yet — the bootstrap branch
///   below (P2-ADJ-0002, the SAME branch that already existed) adopts the document's own claim as its new
///   baseline, exactly as before;
/// * a health-check sandbox (`scheduler::sandbox::Sandbox::create`, a byte copy of the repository under a
///   FRESH, never-reused path every run) *always* takes that same bootstrap branch — [`is_health_sandbox_root`]
///   makes [`path`](FloorIdentity::path) return `None` for one rather than silently accumulate an orphaned
///   machine-store entry per run, but the SECURITY property (a sandbox authenticates) held even without that:
///   a never-seen path key bootstraps regardless;
/// * copying the floor DOCUMENT alone (every AR86/AR88 reproduction) can no longer carry a matching anchor at
///   all, because the anchor was never a file the copy operation could see;
/// * an ordinary process CANNOT reach this path to rewrite `max_known_sequence` the way AR88-C9 measured against
///   `STATE_DIR` — closing C9 not by enforcing the classification harder, but by the classification no longer
///   needing to apply (the file is not inside the governed project tree at all).
///
/// See [`read_project_adoption_floor`]'s own doc for how a governed [`reclassify_adoption_floor`] write is, in
/// addition, bound to the specific CIT that produced it — and why that binding is recorded but not (yet) gated on
/// read, so the two do not silently drift apart while remaining independently correct.
